.* release.gml -- an example GML Starter Set document that also uses the
.* SCRIPT/VS programmable layer: symbols, arithmetic, arrays, macros,
.* conditional text, a loop, and the user-defined tags of the notes tag
.* set in examples/notes/ (:warn, and math: :m, :fml, :fmlref), and two
.* Starter Set tables (Rel 3.2: :rdef, :table, :row, :c, ...), one with
.* rows generated by a .go loop and one with an ARRANGE header, both
.* with a footnote in a cell.
.*
.* Build (one document, one profile per application: notes/profile.gml
.* imbeds starter for the logical device HTML, else dcfstarter):
.*   HTML  ./gmlfmt -tagset examples/notes -to html examples/release.gml
.*         ./gmlfmt -tagset examples/notes -to html -set audience=internal \
.*                  examples/release.gml
.*         (gmlfmt -to html gives gml -set $LDEV=HTML: starter)
.*   PDF   cd examples && ../gmlpass -tagset ./notes -o out/passes release.gml
.*         (the default device: dcfstarter, TeX in hint mode)
.*   all   cd examples && mk   (examples/mkfile: out/)
.*
.* ---- document symbols --------------------------------------------------
.se product = 'Wind Server'
.se major = 4
.se minor = 2
.se version = &major..&minor
.se nextmin = &minor + 1
.se platforms() = 'z/OS'
.se platforms() = 'z/VM'
.se platforms() = 'Linux on Z'
.* -set audience=internal on the command line overrides this default
.if &E'&audience eq 0 .se audience = customer
.*
.* ---- a counter macro: numbers the "changes" in this release ------------
.se nchg = 0
.dm change on
.se nchg = &nchg + 1
:li.&*
.dm off
.*
:gdoc sec='&audience.'
:frontm
:titlep
:title.&product. &version.
:title.Release Notes
:docnum.GC00-&major.&minor.00
:date.
:author.The ANDPUKE Team
:etitlep
:abstract
:p.This document describes &product. Version &version.,
which runs on &platforms(*).. Written at &date..
:toc
:figlist
:tlist
:body
:h1 id=over.Overview
:p.&product. &version. is a maintenance release.
It contains the changes listed in :hdref refid=changes..
:i1.&product.
:i2.version &version.
.* conditional text: .th/.el govern one line each, so skip with .go
.if &audience ne internal .go public
:p.:hp2.Internal readers::ehp2. the code freeze for
&major..&nextmin is planned for next quarter.
.go endaud
...public
:p.Contact your sales representative for the support schedule.
...endaud
:h1 id=changes.Changes in &version.
:p.The following changes were made:
:ol
.change Faster start-up:fn id=fn1.Measured on a cold system.:efn.
.change New :q.quiet:eq. start option
.change Fixes for the problems listed in :figref refid=probs.
:eol
:p.This release has &nchg. changes
(see footnote :fnref refid=fn1.).
:warn level=2.Back up the configuration before you upgrade.
:ewarn.
:p.:hp2.All:ehp2. :hp1.of
this sentence is high:hp3.light:ehp3.ed:ehp1..
Be careful when placing the high:hp3.
light
:ehp3.ed
phrase tags in your text or you :hp1.will:ehp1. get
unexpected spacing results.
:h2 id=cfg.Configuration
:p.The configuration file now accepts these keywords:
:dl
:dt.timeout
:dd.Seconds to wait for a client (default 30).
:i1.timeout keyword
:dt.quiet
:dd.Suppresses the start-up banner.
:i1.quiet keyword
:edl
:p.An example:
:xmp.
  server  main
    timeout 45
    quiet
:exmp.
:fig id=probs frame=box.
:ul compact
:li.WS0012: crash on empty request
:li.WS0019: wrong date in log
:eul
:figcap.Problems fixed in &version.
:efig
.* ---- capacity planning: SCRIPT computes the numbers in the formulas ---
.* .se does integer arithmetic; the .go loop sums the warm-up times
.* 1+2+...+n, so the formulas show values the formatter computed.
.se rate = 250
.se workers = 8
.se peak = &rate * &workers
.se load = 1600
.se util = &load * 100 / &peak
.se shards = 12
.se warm = 0
.se k = 0
...warmup
.se k = &k + 1
.se warm = &warm + &k
.if &k lt &shards .go warmup
.se perday = &peak * 86400
:h2 id=cap.Capacity planning
:p.Each worker thread of &product. &version. serves about
:m.r = &rate.:em. requests per second, so a server with
:m.w = &workers.:em. workers handles at most
:fml id=thru.T = w \cdot r = &workers. \cdot &rate. = &peak.
:efml.
requests per second, or &perday. requests a day.
:p.At start-up the &shards. cache shards are warmed one after another,
and shard :m.k:em. takes :m.k:em. seconds, so the warm-up takes
:fml id=warm.\sum_{k=1}^{&shards.} k = {&shards.(&shards.+1)\over 2} = &warm.\ {\rm s}
:efml.
before the server accepts requests.
:p.Plan the offered load :m.\lambda:em. so that the utilisation
:m.\rho:em. stays at or below 0.8.
With :m.\lambda = &load.:em. requests per second and :m.T:em. from
:fmlref refid=thru.,
:fml id=util.\rho = {\lambda \over T} = {&load. \over &peak.} = 0.&util. \le 0.8
:efml.
If :m.\rho:em. in :fmlref refid=util. rises above the limit, add workers:
by :fmlref refid=thru. each one adds &rate. requests per second.
:tref refid=sizing. gives the utilisation for other numbers of workers.
.* ---- a table whose rows SCRIPT generates: one row per worker count ----
.* utilisation in percent = load * 100 / (rate * workers), integer .se;
.* the first overloaded row gets a footnote (a tag in a symbol value)
:rdef id=szhead cwidths='* * 2*' hp=2 align=c valign=bot.
:rdef id=szrow cwidths='* * 2*' align='r r l'.
:table id=sizing refid=szrow column.
:thd refid=szhead.
:c.Workers:c.Requests per second:c.Utilisation at &load. requests per second
:ethd.
.se w = 0
.se overfn = 0
...sizrow
.se w = &w + 2
.se cap = &rate * &w
.se pct = &load * 100 / &cap
.se verdict = 'within the limit'
.if &pct gt 80 .se verdict = 'too high: add workers'
.if &pct gt 100 .se verdict = 'overloaded'
.se note = ''
.if &pct gt 100 .if &overfn eq 0 .se note = ':fn.Above 100% requests queue up and response times grow without limit.:efn.'
.if &pct gt 100 .se overfn = 1
:row.
:c.&w.:c.&cap.:c.&pct.%, &verdict.&note.
.if &w lt 12 .go sizrow
:tnote.The default configuration has &workers. workers.
:etnote.
:tcap.Utilisation by number of workers
:tdesc.Each row is computed by a SCRIPT loop, at an offered load of
&load. requests per second.
:etable.
:h2.Platforms
:p.Supported platforms:
:sl
:li.&platforms(1)
:li.&platforms(2)
:li.&platforms(3)
:esl
:p.:tref refid=plat. lists the support status of each platform.
.* ---- a header with spanning cells: ARRANGE makes cell 1 two sub-rows
.* high and cell 2 two columns wide, above cells 3 and 4; the new
.* platform's cell gets a footnote
:rdef id=plhead cwidths='2* * 2*' hp=2 align='l c' valign='center bot'
arrange='1 2 2'
arrange='1 3 4'.
:rdef id=plrow cwidths='2* * 2*' align='l c l'.
:table id=plat refid=plrow.
:thd refid=plhead.
:c.Platform:c.Support:c.Since:c.Status
:ethd.
.se i = 0
...platrow
.se i = &i + 1
.se since = &major..0
.if &i eq 3 .se since = &major..&minor
.se status = 'full support'
.se note = ''
.if &i eq 3 .se status = 'new in &version.'
.if &i eq 3 .se note = ':fn.&platforms(3) is supported from release &major..&minor.. Earlier releases may run but are untested.:efn.'
:row.
:c.&platforms(&i)&note.:c.&since.:c.&status.
.if &i lt &platforms(0) .go platrow
:tcap.Supported platforms
:etable.
:warn.All platforms need the latest service level.
:ewarn.
:backm.
:h1.What-so-ever
.fo left
:p. This paragraph should be typed ragged right.  This was originally
achieved with disable typesetting.  I wish there are many many many
After a while, finding that nothing more
happened, she decided on going into the garden at once; but, alas for
poor Alice! when she got to the door, she found she had forgotten the
little golden key, and when she went back to the table for it, she
found she could not possibly reach it: she could see it quite plainly
through the glass, and she tried her best to climb up one of the legs
of the table, but it was too slippery; and when she had tired herself
out with trying, the poor little thing sat down and cried.
.fo on
:p. After a while, finding that nothing more happened, she decided on
going into the garden at once; but, alas for poor Alice! when she got
to the door, she found she had forgotten the little golden key, and
when she went back to the table for it, she found she could not
possibly reach it: she could see it quite plainly through the glass,
and she tried her best to climb up one of the legs of the table, but
it was too slippery; and when she had tired herself out with trying,
the poor little thing sat down and cried.
:note.I wonder is there anything needs to be in the back-matter.
:p.
Some of our favorite fruits are
also some of the most nutritious:
:ul compact.
:li.Apples
:li.Oranges
:li.Pears
:li.Plums
:eul.
:pc.
This is a good reason to enjoy
them often.
:p.One of the great speeches
of all time begins like this:
:lq.
:p.:q.Fourscore and seven years ago
our fathers brought forth on
this continent, a new nation.:eq.
:elq.
:p.It was made by one of the
great presidents of all time.
